2025-06-03 17:14:26,327 - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-d2e1728e-5502-478e-9940-a69a6afda43b', 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4000, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are rewriting fitness/exercise science notes for better accessibility while maintaining scientific rigor. This is part of an interconnected Obsidian knowledge base.\n\nCRITICAL REQUIREMENTS:\n1. NEVER change the note title - this breaks links across the entire library\n2. PRESERVE all existing [[links]] exactly as written - do not modify anything inside [[ ]]\n3. MAINTAIN all scientific accuracy and citations\n4. ADD practical applications if missing\n5. ENSURE all key claims are backed by research citations\n6. Use the exact structure provided below\n\nSTANDARD NOTE STRUCTURE:\n```markdown\n### [Original Title - NEVER CHANGE]\n**ID:** [Keep original ID exactly as is]\n**Created:** [Original date] (Updated: [Current date])\n\n## Quick Takeaway\n[Single sentence bottom line - what users need to know immediately]\n\n## Key Points\n- [3-5 actionable bullet points]\n- [No jargon, specific recommendations]\n- [Focus on practical application]\n\n## Deep Dive\n[Your original detailed content - reorganized with subheadings]\n[Preserve ALL scientific accuracy and mechanisms]\n[Use clear subheadings for complex topics]\n[Include inline citations for key claims]\n\n### [Subheading as needed]\n[Detailed explanations with citations]\n\n## Research Evidence\n[Organize existing citations clearly]\n[Highlight key findings and effect sizes]\n[Note evidence quality levels]\n[Include DOI links where available]\n[Format: Author et al. (Year). Title. Journal, Volume(Issue), Pages. DOI: [link]]\n\n## Practical Applications\n[How to actually implement this - ADD if missing]\n[Specific examples, numbers, protocols]\n[Real-world scenarios]\n\n## Common Mistakes\n⚠️ [What people typically get wrong]\n⚠️ [Misconceptions to avoid]\n⚠️ [Implementation errors]\n\n## Related Concepts\n[PRESERVE ALL EXISTING LINKS EXACTLY]\n[Add contextual phrases around links where helpful]\n[Example: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]" instead of just "[[B2. Protein Requirements]]"]\n\n## Update Log\n- [Date]: [What changed and why]\n```\n\nLINKING PRESERVATION RULES:\n- Keep all [[Note Name]] links exactly as they appear\n- You can add descriptive text around links: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]"\n- Never modify what\'s inside the [[ ]] brackets\n- Never change link destinations\n\nRESEARCH CITATION RULES:\n- Preserve all existing citations\n- Add inline citations for key claims\n- Include DOI links where available\n- Format citations consistently\n- Ensure all major claims are backed by research\n- Add missing citations for important statements\n\nHere\'s the note to rewrite:\n\n### A7. Muscle Physiology Fundamentals\n**ID:** A7-MUSCLE-PHYSIOLOGY\n**Created:** 2025-04-23\n\nUnderstanding skeletal muscle structure and function is key to exercise science.\n\n**Muscle Structure:** Fibers (cells) -> Myofibrils -> Sarcomeres (Actin & Myosin contractile proteins). Connective tissue (Epimysium, Perimysium, Endomysium) transmits force.\n\n**Muscle Contraction (Sliding Filament Theory):** Neural Signal (ACh) -> Excitation (Sarcolemma/T-tubules) -> Calcium Release (SR) -> Cross-Bridge Formation (Actin-Myosin binding via Troponin/Tropomyosin shift) -> Power Stroke (Myosin pulls Actin, uses ATP) -> Detachment (ATP binds Myosin) -> Reactivation (ATP hydrolysis) -> Relaxation (Calcium pumped back to SR).\n\n**Contraction Types:**\n- **Isometric:** Force produced, length constant (e.g., plank).\n- **Concentric:** Muscle shortens while producing force (e.g., lifting phase).\n- **Eccentric:** Muscle lengthens while producing force (e.g., lowering phase). Highest force potential, causes most damage ([[B20. Eccentric Training]]).\n\n**Muscle Fiber Types:** Classified by MHC isoform (I, IIa, IIx).\n- **Type I (Slow-Twitch Oxidative):** Fatigue resistant, aerobic, lower force/power. Recruited first.\n- **Type IIa (Fast-Twitch Oxidative-Glycolytic):** Intermediate properties.\n- **Type IIx (Fast-Twitch Glycolytic):** Highly fatigable, highest force/power, anaerobic. Recruited last/at high intensity.\n- *Ratio largely genetic, some adaptation possible ([[A20. Principle of Specificity (SAID)]]).*\n\n**Key Research:**\n- Huxley AF, Niedergerke R. (1954). Structural changes in muscle during contraction... Nature, 173(4412).\n- Lieber RL, Fridén J. (2000). Functional and clinical significance of skeletal muscle architecture. Muscle & Nerve, 23(11).\n- Schiaffino S, Reggiani C. (2011). Fiber types in mammalian skeletal muscles. Physiological Reviews, 91(4).\n\n**Links:** [[B3. Hypertrophy Training Principles]], [[B4. Strength Training Principles]], [[B19. Isometrics]], [[B20. Eccentric Training]], [[A20. Principle of Specificity (SAID)]], [[G-VII-1. Musculoskeletal Anatomy Overview]], [[G-VII-3. Neurophysiology of Movement]] (Motor Units)'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514'}}
