2025-06-03 17:36:21,752 - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-0f817889-9f44-41ea-9d0c-3484ae45b6a9', 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4000, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are rewriting fitness/exercise science notes for better accessibility while maintaining scientific rigor. This is part of an interconnected Obsidian knowledge base.\n\nCRITICAL REQUIREMENTS:\n1. NEVER change the note title - this breaks links across the entire library\n2. PRESERVE all existing [[links]] exactly as written - do not modify anything inside [[ ]]\n3. MAINTAIN all scientific accuracy and citations\n4. ADD practical applications if missing\n5. ENSURE all key claims are backed by research citations\n6. Use the exact structure provided below\n\nSTANDARD NOTE STRUCTURE:\n```markdown\n### [Original Title - NEVER CHANGE]\n**ID:** [Keep original ID exactly as is]\n**Created:** [Original date] (Updated: [Current date])\n\n## Quick Takeaway\n[Single sentence bottom line - what users need to know immediately]\n\n## Key Points\n- [3-5 actionable bullet points]\n- [No jargon, specific recommendations]\n- [Focus on practical application]\n\n## Deep Dive\n[Your original detailed content - reorganized with subheadings]\n[Preserve ALL scientific accuracy and mechanisms]\n[Use clear subheadings for complex topics]\n[Include inline citations for key claims]\n\n### [Subheading as needed]\n[Detailed explanations with citations]\n\n## Research Evidence\n[Organize existing citations clearly]\n[Highlight key findings and effect sizes]\n[Note evidence quality levels]\n[Include DOI links where available]\n[Format: Author et al. (Year). Title. Journal, Volume(Issue), Pages. DOI: [link]]\n\n## Practical Applications\n[How to actually implement this - ADD if missing]\n[Specific examples, numbers, protocols]\n[Real-world scenarios]\n\n## Common Mistakes\n⚠️ [What people typically get wrong]\n⚠️ [Misconceptions to avoid]\n⚠️ [Implementation errors]\n\n## Related Concepts\n[PRESERVE ALL EXISTING LINKS EXACTLY]\n[Add contextual phrases around links where helpful]\n[Example: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]" instead of just "[[B2. Protein Requirements]]"]\n\n## Update Log\n- [Date]: [What changed and why]\n```\n\nLINKING PRESERVATION RULES:\n- Keep all [[Note Name]] links exactly as they appear\n- You can add descriptive text around links: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]"\n- Never modify what\'s inside the [[ ]] brackets\n- Never change link destinations\n\nRESEARCH CITATION RULES:\n- Preserve all existing citations\n- Add inline citations for key claims\n- Include DOI links where available\n- Format citations consistently\n- Ensure all major claims are backed by research\n- Add missing citations for important statements\n\nHere\'s the note to rewrite:\n\n**ID:** C23-SUPPLEMENT-SAFETY **Created:** 2025-04-26 _(Content Revised)_\n\nEnsuring supplement safety and quality is paramount due to regulatory differences compared to pharmaceuticals and documented issues with contamination and inaccurate labeling [].\n\n**Regulatory Landscape (Varies by Country, e.g., USA - DSHEA 1994):**\n\n- Supplements are generally regulated as foods, not drugs.\n- Manufacturers are responsible for safety and labeling _before_ marketing; pre-market approval by regulatory bodies (like the FDA in the US) is typically **not** required.\n- Regulatory agencies often act _reactively_ to address safety issues after products are on the market.\n- Claims are restricted (structure/function claims allowed, disease claims prohibited without approval).\n\n**Potential Risks:**\n\n- **Contamination:** A significant concern. Products may contain undeclared substances, including:\n    - Banned substances (anabolic steroids, stimulants, diuretics - major risk for tested athletes [[P2. Performance Enhancement Ethics]]).\n    - Prescription medications.\n    - Heavy metals or other toxins.\n    - Allergens.\n- **Inaccurate Labeling:** Actual ingredient amounts can differ substantially from the label (either less or more).\n- **Adverse Effects:** Ingredients themselves might cause side effects or interact with medications.\n- **Lack of Efficacy:** Many products make claims unsupported by scientific evidence [[J3. Misinformation in Fitness]].\n\n**Minimizing Risk - Quality Control:**\n\n- **Third-Party Certification:** The most reliable way to reduce risk. Independent organizations test products for contaminants, banned substances, and verify label claims/potency. Look for reputable certification marks on products:\n    - **NSF Certified for Sport®:** Rigorous testing for 270+ banned substances, contaminants, and label verification. Widely recognized in sports.\n    - **Informed Sport / Informed Choice:** Batch tests products for substances banned by WADA.\n    - **BSCG (Banned Substances Control Group):** Tests for banned substances in sport plus prescription/OTC drugs.\n    - **USP Verified:** Verifies ingredient identity, potency, purity, and good manufacturing practices (less focus on banned substances specifically).\n- **Reputable Brands:** Choose well-established companies adhering to Good Manufacturing Practices (GMPs).\n- **Simple Formulas:** Be cautious with proprietary blends hiding ingredient amounts.\n- **Research:** Investigate evidence for ingredients [[C21. Evidence-Based Supplements (Group A)]], [[C22. Supplements with LimitedMixed Evidence (Group B)]].\n- **Professional Guidance:** Consult with knowledgeable sports dietitians or physicians [[P1. Ethical Training and Coaching]].\n- **Check Prohibited Lists:** Athletes must verify all substances against the current WADA (or relevant governing body) Prohibited List. Remember _strict liability_ applies.\n\n**Key Message:** No supplement is 100% risk-free regarding contamination or adverse effects. Third-party certification significantly _reduces_ but does not eliminate risk.\n\n**Key Research/Resources:**\n\n- Maughan, R.J., et al. (2018). IOC consensus statement: dietary supplements and the high-performance athlete. _British Journal of Sports Medicine_, 52(7), 439-455. [Available Online](https://pinesnutrition.org/the-ioc-consensus-statement-on-dietary-supplements-2/) \xa0\n    \n- Cohen, P.A., et al. (2022). Presence and Quantity of Botanical Ingredients With Purported Performance-Enhancing Properties in Sports Supplements. _JAMA Network Open_, 5(7), e2223388. (Example research highlighting labeling issues) \xa0\n    \n- U.S. Food & Drug Administration (FDA). Information for Consumers on Using Dietary Supplements. (Official resource)\n- NSF Certified for Sport® (Website/Database)\n- Informed Sport / Informed Choice (Website/Database)\n- BSCG (Website/Database)\n- World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA). (Website - Prohibited List)\n\n**Links:** [[C20. Supplements (Overview & Framework)]], [[C21. Evidence-Based Supplements (Group A)]], [[C22. Supplements with LimitedMixed Evidence (Group B)]], [[J3. Misinformation in Fitness]], [[P2. Performance Enhancement Ethics]]'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514'}}
