2025-06-03 17:52:56,674 - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-244cc851-14f4-44f8-92a8-be8ed186211c', 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4000, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are rewriting fitness/exercise science notes for better accessibility while maintaining scientific rigor. This is part of an interconnected Obsidian knowledge base.\n\nCRITICAL REQUIREMENTS:\n1. NEVER change the note title - this breaks links across the entire library\n2. PRESERVE all existing [[links]] exactly as written - do not modify anything inside [[ ]]\n3. MAINTAIN all scientific accuracy and citations\n4. ADD practical applications if missing\n5. ENSURE all key claims are backed by research citations\n6. Use the exact structure provided below\n\nSTANDARD NOTE STRUCTURE:\n```markdown\n### [Original Title - NEVER CHANGE]\n**ID:** [Keep original ID exactly as is]\n**Created:** [Original date] (Updated: [Current date])\n\n## Quick Takeaway\n[Single sentence bottom line - what users need to know immediately]\n\n## Key Points\n- [3-5 actionable bullet points]\n- [No jargon, specific recommendations]\n- [Focus on practical application]\n\n## Deep Dive\n[Your original detailed content - reorganized with subheadings]\n[Preserve ALL scientific accuracy and mechanisms]\n[Use clear subheadings for complex topics]\n[Include inline citations for key claims]\n\n### [Subheading as needed]\n[Detailed explanations with citations]\n\n## Research Evidence\n[Organize existing citations clearly]\n[Highlight key findings and effect sizes]\n[Note evidence quality levels]\n[Include DOI links where available]\n[Format: Author et al. (Year). Title. Journal, Volume(Issue), Pages. DOI: [link]]\n\n## Practical Applications\n[How to actually implement this - ADD if missing]\n[Specific examples, numbers, protocols]\n[Real-world scenarios]\n\n## Common Mistakes\n⚠️ [What people typically get wrong]\n⚠️ [Misconceptions to avoid]\n⚠️ [Implementation errors]\n\n## Related Concepts\n[PRESERVE ALL EXISTING LINKS EXACTLY]\n[Add contextual phrases around links where helpful]\n[Example: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]" instead of just "[[B2. Protein Requirements]]"]\n\n## Update Log\n- [Date]: [What changed and why]\n```\n\nLINKING PRESERVATION RULES:\n- Keep all [[Note Name]] links exactly as they appear\n- You can add descriptive text around links: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]"\n- Never modify what\'s inside the [[ ]] brackets\n- Never change link destinations\n\nRESEARCH CITATION RULES:\n- Preserve all existing citations\n- Add inline citations for key claims\n- Include DOI links where available\n- Format citations consistently\n- Ensure all major claims are backed by research\n- Add missing citations for important statements\n\nHere\'s the note to rewrite:\n\n### E12. Lactate Threshold Training\n**ID:** E12-LACTATE-THRESHOLD-TRAINING\n**Created:** 2025-04-23\n\nLactate threshold (LT) training aims to improve the exercise intensity at which lactate accumulation significantly increases (LT1/Aerobic Threshold) and/or the maximal lactate steady state (MLSS or LT2/Anaerobic Threshold). Improving LT allows athletes to sustain higher intensities/speeds aerobically for longer periods.\n\n**Physiological Basis (related to [[A6. Energy Systems (Detailed)]]):** LT represents balance between lactate production (glycolysis) and clearance (oxidation/gluconeogenesis). Training improves clearance capacity (↑mitochondria, ↑MCT transporters) and tolerance.\n\n**Effective Training Methods:**\n- **Tempo/Threshold Training:** Sustained efforts at/slightly below LT2/MLSS intensity (~80-90% HRmax, Zone 3-4, RPE "comfortably hard"). Duration 20-60 min continuous or long intervals (e.g., 2-4 x 10-20 min) with short recovery.\n- **Sweet Spot Training (Cycling):** Intensity slightly below FTP/LT2 (~88-94% FTP), allows substantial volume accumulation at challenging but sustainable intensity.\n- **Extensive Aerobic Training (LISS - [[E2. Steady-State Cardio (LISS - Low-Intensity Steady State)]]):** High volume low-intensity training improves aerobic base and LT1, supporting threshold training.\n\n**Programming:** Typically 1-2 sessions/week. Intensity needs accurate determination (lab tests, field tests like FTP, HR/RPE estimation).\n\n**Importance:** LT (esp. LT2/MLSS) often better predictor of endurance performance than VO2max alone, representing highest sustainable aerobic intensity.\n\n**Key Research:**\n- Faude O, Kindermann W, Meyer T. (2009). Lactate threshold concepts: how valid are they?. Sports Medicine, 39(6), 469-490.\n- Seiler S, Tønnessen E. (2009). Intervals, thresholds, and long slow distance: the role of intensity and duration in endurance training. Sportscience, 13, 32-53.\n- Jones AM, Carter H. (2000). The effect of endurance training on parameters of aerobic fitness. Sports Medicine, 29(6), 373-386.\n\n**Links:** [[A6. Energy Systems (Detailed)]], [[E1. Aerobic vs. Anaerobic Training]], [[E2. Steady-State Cardio (LISS - Low-Intensity Steady State)]], [[E4. Interval Training (General)]], [[E11. VO2 Max Training]], [[E13. Heart Rate Zone Training]]'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514'}}
