⚠️ [What people typically get wrong]\n⚠️ [Misconceptions to avoid]\n⚠️ [Implementation errors]\n\n## Related Concepts\n[PRESERVE ALL EXISTING LINKS EXACTLY]\n[Add contextual phrases around links where helpful]\n[Example: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]" instead of just "[[B2. Protein Requirements]]"]\n\n## Update Log\n- [Date]: [What changed and why]\n```\n\nLINKING PRESERVATION RULES:\n- Keep all [[Note Name]] links exactly as they appear\n- You can add descriptive text around links: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]"\n- Never modify what\'s inside the [[ ]] brackets\n- Never change link destinations\n\nRESEARCH CITATION RULES:\n- Preserve all existing citations\n- Add inline citations for key claims\n- Include DOI links where available\n- Format citations consistently\n- Ensure all major claims are backed by research\n- Add missing citations for important statements\n\nHere\'s the note to rewrite:\n\n# A31. Force-Velocity Profiling\n\n**ID:** A31-FORCE-VELOCITY-PROFILING\n**Created:** 2025-04-26 (Updated: 2025-01-14)\n\n## Quick Takeaway\n\nForce-velocity profiling in biomechanics involves understanding the inverse relationship between force and velocity in muscle contractions, with specific training methods to optimize performance across the force-velocity spectrum.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Force-velocity relationship shows inverse correlation in muscle function\n- Maximum force output increases by 20-30% with strength training\n- Maximum velocity improves by 15-25% with speed training\n- Power output enhances by 25-35% with optimal training\n- Performance efficiency increases by 20-40% with proper profiling\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Force-Velocity Components\n\n**Force Characteristics:**\n\n1. **Maximum Force**\n\n   - Isometric strength\n   - Concentric force\n   - Eccentric force\n   - Force development\n   - Force maintenance\n\n2. **Force Production**\n   - Rate of force development\n   - Force-time characteristics\n   - Force application\n   - Force direction\n   - Force control\n\n**Velocity Characteristics:**\n\n1. **Maximum Velocity**\n\n   - Movement speed\n   - Acceleration\n   - Deceleration\n   - Velocity control\n   - Speed maintenance\n\n2. **Velocity Production**\n   - Rate of velocity development\n   - Velocity-time characteristics\n   - Velocity application\n   - Velocity direction\n   - Velocity control\n\n## Research Evidence\n\n- Bobbert, M.F., Lindberg, K., Bjørnsen, T., Solberg, P., & Paulsen, G. (2023). The force–velocity profile for jumping: what it is and what it is not. Medicine & Science in Sports & Exercise, 55(7), 1241–1249. https://doi.org/10.1249/MSS.0000000000003147\n\n- Lindberg, K., Solberg, P., Bjørnsen, T., Helland, C., Rønnestad, B., Frank, M.T., Haugen, T., Østerås, S., Kristoffersen, M., Midttun, M., Sæland, F., & Paulsen, G. (2021). Force-velocity profiling in athletes: Reliability and agreement across methods. PLoS ONE, 16(2), e0245791. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0245791\n\n- Samozino, P., Rejc, E., Di Prampero, P.E., Belli, A., & Morin, J.B. (2012). Optimal force–velocity profile in ballistic movements—altius: citius or fortius? Medicine & Science in Sports & Exercise, 44(2), 313–322. https://doi.org/10.1249/MSS.0b013e31822d757a\n\n## Practical Applications\n\n**Assessment Protocols:**\n\n1. **Force Assessment**\n\n   - Maximum strength tests\n   - Force development tests\n   - Power tests\n   - Force application tests\n   - Force control tests\n\n2. **Velocity Assessment**\n   - Speed tests\n   - Acceleration tests\n   - Power tests\n   - Velocity application tests\n   - Velocity control tests\n\n**Training Implementation:**\n\n1. **Force Development**\n\n   - Strength exercises\n   - Power exercises\n   - Force control exercises\n   - Progressive overload\n   - Technical emphasis\n\n2. **Velocity Development**\n   - Speed exercises\n   - Power exercises\n   - Velocity control exercises\n   - Progressive overload\n   - Technical emphasis\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n⚠️ **Training only one end of the spectrum** - Balance is crucial for optimal function\n\n⚠️ **Ignoring individual differences** - Each athlete requires specific attention\n\n⚠️ **Poor exercise selection** - Exercises must match force-velocity requirements\n\n⚠️ **Inadequate progression** - Systematic development is essential\n\n## Related Concepts\n\nForce-velocity profiling directly informs [[A32. Power Profiling and Testing]] and [[A33. Motor Learning and Skill Acquisition]]. It\'s crucial for [[X12. Vertical Jump Training]] and [[X11. Sprinting]]. Modern assessment tools are discussed in [[VIII-3. Wearable Technology (Fitness Trackers, Smartwatches, HRMs)]].\n\n## Update Log\n\n- 2025-01-14: Created with practical applications and common mistakes section, maintained scientific accuracy and citations\n'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514'}}
