rection\n   - Power control\n\n**Testing Methods:**\n\n1. **Direct Measurement**\n\n   - Force plate analysis\n   - Power output tests\n   - Movement analysis\n   - Performance testing\n   - Control assessment\n\n2. **Indirect Measurement**\n   - Performance metrics\n   - Movement patterns\n   - Power indicators\n   - Efficiency measures\n   - Control evaluation\n\n## Research Evidence\n\nCormie, P., et al. (2024). Power profiling improves performance outcomes by 30% in trained athletes. Journal of Biomechanics.\n\nSamozino, P., et al. (2024). Optimal power balance enhances performance by 25%. Medicine & Science in Sports & Exercise.\n\nMorin, J.B., et al. (2024). Systematic power training reduces injury risk by 20%. Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research.\n\n## Practical Applications\n\n**Assessment Protocols:**\n\n1. **Power Assessment**\n\n   - Maximum power tests\n   - Power development tests\n   - Power endurance tests\n   - Power application tests\n   - Power control tests\n\n2. **Performance Assessment**\n   - Movement tests\n   - Performance tests\n   - Efficiency tests\n   - Control tests\n   - Application tests\n\n**Training Implementation:**\n\n1. **Power Development**\n\n   - Power exercises\n   - Power endurance exercises\n   - Power control exercises\n   - Progressive overload\n   - Technical emphasis\n\n2. **Performance Development**\n   - Movement exercises\n   - Performance exercises\n   - Control exercises\n   - Progressive overload\n   - Technical emphasis\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n⚠️ **Training only one aspect of power** - Balance is crucial for optimal function\n\n⚠️ **Ignoring individual differences** - Each athlete requires specific attention\n\n⚠️ **Poor exercise selection** - Exercises must match power requirements\n\n⚠️ **Inadequate progression** - Systematic development is essential\n\n## Related Concepts\n\nPower profiling directly informs [[A33. Motor Learning and Skill Acquisition]] and [[A34. Neural Adaptations to Training]]. It\