⚠️ [What people typically get wrong]\n⚠️ [Misconceptions to avoid]\n⚠️ [Implementation errors]\n\n## Related Concepts\n[PRESERVE ALL EXISTING LINKS EXACTLY]\n[Add contextual phrases around links where helpful]\n[Example: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]" instead of just "[[B2. Protein Requirements]]"]\n\n## Update Log\n- [Date]: [What changed and why]\n```\n\nLINKING PRESERVATION RULES:\n- Keep all [[Note Name]] links exactly as they appear\n- You can add descriptive text around links: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]"\n- Never modify what\'s inside the [[ ]] brackets\n- Never change link destinations\n\nRESEARCH CITATION RULES:\n- Preserve all existing citations\n- Add inline citations for key claims\n- Include DOI links where available\n- Format citations consistently\n- Ensure all major claims are backed by research\n- Add missing citations for important statements\n\nHere\'s the note to rewrite:\n\n# A30. Biomechanics: Stability vs. Mobility\n\n## Conceptual Overview\n\nThe relationship between stability and mobility in biomechanics represents a fundamental principle of human movement, where different joints require specific ratios of stability and mobility to function optimally. This concept is crucial for understanding movement patterns, injury prevention, and performance optimization [[A31. Force-Velocity Profiling]], [[A32. Power Profiling and Testing]].\n\n## Theoretical Foundations\n\n- **Joint-by-Joint Approach:** A systematic framework that identifies the primary need (stability or mobility) for each major joint in the kinetic chain. This approach helps in understanding movement patterns and designing appropriate training interventions.\n\n  - **Mobility-Dominant Joints:** Ankle, hip, thoracic spine, and glenohumeral joint require greater mobility for optimal function.\n  - **Stability-Dominant Joints:** Knee, lumbar spine, and scapulothoracic joint require greater stability for optimal function.\n\n- **Stability Mechanisms:**\n\n  - **Active Stability:** Provided by muscular control and coordination, particularly through the deep stabilizer muscles.\n  - **Passive Stability:** Provided by joint capsules, ligaments, and bony structures.\n  - **Neural Control:** Proprioceptive feedback and motor control patterns that maintain joint position and movement quality.\n\n- **Mobility Components:**\n  - **Joint Range of Motion:** The available movement at a joint in all planes.\n  - **Movement Quality:** The efficiency and control of movement through the available range.\n  - **Motor Control:** The ability to coordinate movement patterns effectively.\n\n## Practical Applications\n\n- **Assessment Protocols [[A50. Performance Testing and Evaluation]]:** Systematic evaluation of joint function using movement screens and specific tests to identify limitations in stability or mobility.\n- **Training Progression:** Development of appropriate stability or mobility based on joint-specific needs and individual requirements.\n- **Movement Pattern Integration:** Incorporation of stability and mobility training into functional movement patterns.\n- **Injury Prevention:** Addressing stability and mobility deficits to reduce injury risk and improve movement quality.\n- **Performance Enhancement:** Optimizing the stability-mobility relationship for improved athletic performance.\n\n## Research Support\n\n- Cook, G., Burton, L., & Hoogenboom, B. (2006). Pre-participation screening: the use of fundamental movements as an assessment of function - part 1. North American Journal of Sports Physical Therapy, 1(2), 62–72. https://doi.org/10.26603/001c.8782\n\n- McGill, S. M. (2010). Core training: Evidence translating to better performance and injury prevention. Strength and Conditioning Journal, 32(3), 33–46. https://doi.org/10.1519/SSC.0b013e3181df4521\n\n- Behm, D. G., & Anderson, K. (2006). The role of instability with resistance training. Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research, 20(3), 716–722. https://doi.org/10.1519/R-18795.1\n\n- Hodges, P. W., & Richardson, C. A. (1996). Inefficient muscular stabilization of the lumbar spine associated with low back pain. Spine, 21(22), 2640-2650. https://doi.org/10.1097/00007632-199611150-00014\n\n- Kibler, W. B., et al. (2013). The role of the scapula in athletic shoulder function. The American Journal of Sports Medicine, 41(4), 936-944. https://doi.org/10.1177/0363546513481109\n\n## Limitations and Considerations\n\n- Individual variability in joint structure and function requires personalized assessment and programming.\n- The relationship between stability and mobility is dynamic and can change with training, injury, or aging.\n- Overemphasis on either stability or mobility can lead to movement dysfunction and increased injury risk.\n- The effectiveness of stability and mobility training depends on proper exercise selection and progression.\n\n## Connections to Existing Knowledge\n\nDirectly informs [[A31. Force-Velocity Profiling]], [[A32. Power Profiling and Testing]], and [[A33. Motor Learning and Skill Acquisition]]. Crucial for [[X12. Vertical Jump Training]] and [[X11. Sprinting]]. Modern assessment tools are discussed in [[VIII-3. Wearable Technology (Fitness Trackers, Smartwatches, HRMs)]]. Relates to [[IX-7. Pain Science in Exercise & Rehabilitation]] and [[IX-6 Exercise Contraindications]].\n\n## Key References\n\n- Cook, G., Burton, L., & Hoogenboom, B. (2006). Pre-participation screening: the use of fundamental movements as an assessment of function - part 1. North American Journal of Sports Physical Therapy, 1(2), 62–72.\n\n- McGill, S. M. (2010). Core training: Evidence translating to better performance and injury prevention. Strength and Conditioning Journal, 32(3), 33–46.\n\n- Behm, D. G., & Anderson, K. (2006). The role of instability with resistance training. Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research, 20(3), 716–722.\n\n- Hodges, P. W., & Richardson, C. A. (1996). Inefficient muscular stab