tps://doi.org/[DOI]\n- Key finding: [Specific measurable result or main conclusion]\n- Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]  \n- Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n\n**Alternative format with PMID:**\n**Author et al. (Year).** Title. *Journal*, Volume(Issue), Pages. PMID: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/[PMID]\n- Key finding: [Specific measurable result or main conclusion]\n- Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]\n- Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n\n[REQUIREMENTS:]\n- Include ONLY citations with working DOI or PMID links\n- Verify links work before including them\n- Focus on 3-5 highest quality, most relevant studies\n- Remove any citations that cannot be verified with working links\n\n## Practical Applications\n[How to actually implement this - ADD if missing]\n[Specific examples, numbers, protocols]\n[Real-world scenarios]\n\n## Common Mistakes\n⚠️ [What people typically get wrong]\n⚠️ [Misconceptions to avoid]\n⚠️ [Implementation errors]\n\n## Related Concepts\n[PRESERVE ALL EXISTING LINKS EXACTLY]\n[Add contextual phrases around links where helpful]\n[Example: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]" instead of just "[[B2. Protein Requirements]]"]\n\n## Update Log\n- [Date]: [What changed and why]\n```\n\nLINKING PRESERVATION RULES:\n- Keep all [[Note Name]] links exactly as they appear\n- You can add descriptive text around links: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]"\n- Never modify what\'s inside the [[ ]] brackets\n- Never change link destinations\n\nRESEARCH CITATION RULES:\n- CRITICAL: Every citation MUST have a working DOI or PMID link - NO EXCEPTIONS\n- Use the ENHANCED RESEARCH EVIDENCE FORMAT with mandatory links\n- Format: **Author et al. (Year).** Title. *Journal*, Volume(Issue), Pages. DOI: https://doi.org/[DOI]\n- Follow each citation with:\n  - Key finding: [Specific measurable result]\n  - Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]\n  - Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n- VERIFY all DOI/PMID links work before including them\n- If you cannot find working links, do NOT include the citation\n- Better to have 2-3 citations with working links than 5 without links\n- Focus on highest quality studies with verifiable sources\n- Do NOT make up or guess DOI/PMID numbers\n\nHere\'s the note to rewrite:\n\n### A1. Energy Balance\n**ID:** A1-ENERGY-BALANCE\n**Created:** 2025-04-23 (Updated: 2025-01-08)\n\n## Quick Takeaway\nEnergy balance—the relationship between calories consumed and calories burned—determines whether you gain, lose, or maintain weight according to fundamental physics.\n\n## Key Points\n- Eat more calories than you burn = weight gain (surplus)\n- Burn more calories than you eat = weight loss (deficit)\n- Match calories in with calories out = weight maintenance\n- Athletes need sufficient energy availability (30-35+ kcal/kg fat-free mass daily) to maintain health and performance\n- Body composition changes depend on training stimulus and protein intake, not just energy balance\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nEnergy balance describes the relationship between energy intake (EI - calories consumed) and energy expenditure (EE - calories burned). This relationship dictates changes in body mass according to the First Law of Thermodynamics, which states that energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed.\n\n### The Three States of Energy Balance\n\n**Caloric Surplus (EI > EE):** Results in weight gain through storage of excess energy. While this is primarily stored as fat, lean tissue can also be gained when combined with appropriate stimulus like resistance training and adequate protein intake (Hall et al., 2012).\n\n**Caloric Deficit (EI < EE):** Leads to weight loss through mobilization of stored energy. The body preferentially uses fat stores, but lean tissue loss can occur without adequate protein intake and resistance training stimulus (Müller et al., 2018).\n\n**Energy Balance (EI = EE):** Results in weight maintenance, with energy intake matching expenditure over time.\n\n### Energy Availability for Athletes\n\nEnergy Availability (EA) represents a more sophisticated concept than simple energy balance, particularly relevant for athletes. EA describes the dietary energy remaining for physiological functions after accounting for exercise energy expenditure, normalized to fat-free mass:\n\n**EA = (Energy Intake - Exercise Energy Expenditure) / Fat-Free Mass**\n\nLow energy availability (<30-35 kcal/kg FFM/day) significantly impairs both health and performance markers (Loucks et al., 2011).\n\n## Research Evidence\n\n- Hall KD, et al. (2012). Energy balance and its components: implications for body weight regulation. American Journal of Clinical Nutrition, 95(4), 989-994.\n- Müller MJ, et al. (2018). Advances in the understanding of specific metabolic rates of major organs and tissues in humans. Current Opinion in Clinical Nutrition and Metabolic Care, 21(5), 343-350.\n- Loucks AB, et al. (2011). Low energy availability in athletes. Journal of Sports Sciences, 29 Suppl 1, S7-S15.\n\n## Practical Applications\n\n- **Weight Loss:** Create a deficit of 300-500 calories daily for sustainable fat loss of 0.5-1 lb per week\n- **Weight Gain:** Maintain a surplus of 200-500 calories daily, combined with resistance training for lean mass gains\n- **Athlete Monitoring:** Calculate energy availability weekly, ensuring intake stays above 35 kcal/kg FFM/day\n- **Body Recomposition:** Use small deficits (200-300 calories) with high protein intake and resistance training\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n⚠️ Assuming all weight gain/loss is fat—body composition depends on training and nutrition quality\n⚠️ Ignoring energy availability in favor of simple calorie counting, especially for active individuals\n⚠️ Expecting linear weight changes—water retention, hormones, and adaptive metabolism affect daily fluctuations\n⚠️ Focusing solely on scale weight rather than body composition changes\n\n## Related Concepts\n\nUnderstanding total daily energy expenditure [[A2. Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) and Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)]] helps determine appropriate caloric targets for different goals [[C1. Caloric Targets for Different Goals]]. These principles form the foundation for evidence-based weight loss [[D1. Evidence-Based Weight Loss Principles]] and understanding energy deficiency issues in sport [[I3. Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (RED-S)]].\n\n## Update Log\n- 2025-01-08: Restructured for accessibility, added practical applications and common mistakes sections, maintained all scientific accuracy and citations'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a fitness science expert helping to improve the accessibility and clarity of exercise science notes while maintaining scientific accuracy.', 'temperature': 0.7}}
