tps://doi.org/[DOI]\n- Key finding: [Specific measurable result or main conclusion]\n- Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]  \n- Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n\n**Alternative format with PMID:**\n**Author et al. (Year).** Title. *Journal*, Volume(Issue), Pages. PMID: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/[PMID]\n- Key finding: [Specific measurable result or main conclusion]\n- Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]\n- Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n\n[REQUIREMENTS:]\n- Include ONLY citations with working DOI or PMID links\n- Verify links work before including them\n- Focus on 3-5 highest quality, most relevant studies\n- Remove any citations that cannot be verified with working links\n\n## Practical Applications\n[How to actually implement this - ADD if missing]\n[Specific examples, numbers, protocols]\n[Real-world scenarios]\n\n## Common Mistakes\n⚠️ [What people typically get wrong]\n⚠️ [Misconceptions to avoid]\n⚠️ [Implementation errors]\n\n## Related Concepts\n[PRESERVE ALL EXISTING LINKS EXACTLY]\n[Add contextual phrases around links where helpful]\n[Example: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]" instead of just "[[B2. Protein Requirements]]"]\n\n## Update Log\n- [Date]: [What changed and why]\n```\n\nLINKING PRESERVATION RULES:\n- Keep all [[Note Name]] links exactly as they appear\n- You can add descriptive text around links: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]"\n- Never modify what\'s inside the [[ ]] brackets\n- Never change link destinations\n\nRESEARCH CITATION RULES:\n- CRITICAL: Every citation MUST have a working DOI or PMID link - NO EXCEPTIONS\n- Use the ENHANCED RESEARCH EVIDENCE FORMAT with mandatory links\n- Format: **Author et al. (Year).** Title. *Journal*, Volume(Issue), Pages. DOI: https://doi.org/[DOI]\n- Follow each citation with:\n  - Key finding: [Specific measurable result]\n  - Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]\n  - Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n- VERIFY all DOI/PMID links work before including them\n- If you cannot find working links, do NOT include the citation\n- Better to have 2-3 citations with working links than 5 without links\n- Focus on highest quality studies with verifiable sources\n- Do NOT make up or guess DOI/PMID numbers\n\nHere\'s the note to rewrite:\n\n### A3. Macronutrients\n**ID:** A3-MACRONUTRIENTS\n**Created:** 2025-04-23 (Updated: 2025-01-27)\n\n## Quick Takeaway\nThe three macronutrients (protein, carbohydrates, and fats) provide energy and essential building blocks for your body, with optimal ratios varying based on your activity level, body composition goals, and individual needs.\n\n## Key Points\n- **Protein**: Aim for 1.2-2.2g/kg body weight if active, focusing on complete proteins with all essential amino acids\n- **Carbohydrates**: Adjust intake based on activity (3-12g/kg/day), prioritizing complex carbs and fiber\n- **Fats**: Keep at 20-35% of total calories, emphasizing unsaturated fats while limiting trans and saturated fats\n- **Distribution matters**: Balance all three macros rather than eliminating any single category\n- **Timing counts**: Protein throughout the day, carbs around workouts, healthy fats for satiety\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Protein: The Building Block Macronutrient\nProtein provides approximately 4 calories per gram and serves as the primary structural component of muscle tissue, enzymes, hormones, and immune cells. The body requires 9 essential amino acids (EAAs) that must be obtained through diet, as the body cannot synthesize them independently.\n\n**Metabolic Impact**: Protein has the highest thermic effect of food (TEF) at 20-30%, meaning your body burns significant calories just digesting and processing protein (Westerterp, 2004).\n\n**Quality Assessment**: Protein quality is evaluated using the Digestible Indispensable Amino Acid Score (DIAAS), which considers both essential amino acid content and digestibility. Leucine, requiring 2-3g per meal, serves as the primary trigger for muscle protein synthesis (MPS).\n\n### Carbohydrates: The Primary Fuel Source\nCarbohydrates provide approximately 4 calories per gram and serve as the preferred fuel source for the central nervous system and moderate-to-high intensity exercise. They\'re stored as glycogen in muscles and liver, and help spare protein from being used as fuel.\n\n**Types and Functions**: \n- Simple carbohydrates (sugars) provide quick energy\n- Complex carbohydrates (starches) offer sustained energy release\n- Dietary fiber (soluble and insoluble) supports digestive health and satiety\n\n**TEF**: Moderate at 5-10% of calories consumed.\n\n### Fats: The Energy-Dense Essential Nutrient\nFats provide approximately 9 calories per gram, making them the most energy-dense macronutrient. They\'re crucial for cell membrane structure, hormone synthesis, and absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K).\n\n**Essential Requirements**: The body requires essential fatty acids (EFAs) including Omega-6 (linoleic acid) and Omega-3 (alpha-linolenic acid, EPA, DHA). These cannot be synthesized by the body and must come from food sources.\n\n**TEF**: Lowest at 0-3% of calories consumed.\n\n## Research Evidence\n\n**Protein Requirements During Energy Restriction:**\nHelms et al. (2014) demonstrated that resistance-trained lean athletes require 1.8-2.7g/kg body weight during caloric restriction to preserve lean mass. DOI: 10.1123/ijsnem.2013-0054\n\n**Macronutrient Distribution for Body Composition:**\nAragon et al. (2017) confirmed that total caloric intake matters more than specific macronutrient ratios for weight loss, though adequate protein intake (1.2-2.2g/kg) optimizes body composition changes. DOI: 10.1186/s12970-017-0174-y\n\n**Carbohydrate Needs for Athletes:**\nBurke et al. (2011) established carbohydrate requirements ranging from 3-12g/kg/day based on training demands, with higher intakes supporting glycogen replenishment and performance. DOI: 10.1080/02640414.2011.589469\n\n**Protein and Exercise Interactions:**\nJäger et al. (2017) confirmed that active individuals benefit from protein intakes of 1.4-2.0g/kg/day, distributed throughout the day in 20-40g servings. DOI: 10.1186/s12970-017-0177-8\n\n## Practical Applications\n\n**Daily Protein Distribution:**\n- Consume 20-40g protein per meal, spaced 3-4 hours apart\n- Include 2-3g leucine per serving (found in 25g whey protein or 30g chicken breast)\n- Prioritize complete proteins: eggs, dairy, meat, fish, quinoa, soy\n\n**Carbohydrate Timing:**\n- Pre-workout: 1-4g/kg body weight 1-4 hours before exercise\n- Post-workout: 1-1.2g/kg within 30-60 minutes after training\n- Daily needs: Sedentary (3-5g/kg), Moderate activity (5-7g/kg), High activity (6-10g/kg), Ultra-endurance (8-12g/kg)\n\n**Fat Intake Guidelines:**\n- Minimum: 0.5-0.6g/kg body weight for essential functions\n- Optimal: 20-35% of total daily calories\n- Sources: Nuts, seeds, avocados, olive oil, fatty fish\n- Limit saturated fats to <10% of total calories\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n⚠️ **Eliminating entire macronutrient groups** - All three macros serve essential functions and shouldn\'t be completely eliminated\n\n⚠️ **Focusing only on protein while ignoring carbs and fats** - This can impair performance, hormone production, and nutrient absorption\n\n⚠️ **Consuming all daily protein in 1-2 large meals** - Spreading protein throughout the day optimizes muscle protein synthesis\n\n⚠️ **Choosing fats based solely on calories** - Trans fats and excessive saturated fats can negatively impact health regardless of caloric content\n\n⚠️ **Ignoring fiber intake** - Aiming for 25-35g daily fiber supports satiety, digestive health, and blood sugar control\n\n## Related Concepts\n\nUnderstanding macronutrients builds on the foundation of energy balance principles [[A1. Energy Balance]] and helps determine your total daily energy needs [[A2. Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) and Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)]]. While macronutrients provide the caloric framework, don\'t overlook the importance of vitamins and minerals [[A4. Micronutrients]]. \n\nFor muscle building goals, specific protein requirements [[B2. Protein Requirements for Muscle Growth]] become crucial. The practical application involves determining your optimal macronutrient distribution [[C2. Macronutrient Distribution]] and implementing strategic nutrient timing [[C3. Nutrient Timing]] to maximize results.\n\n## Update Log\n- 2025-01-27: Restructured for accessibility, added practical applications, expanded research evidence with DOIs, included common mistakes section'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a fitness science expert helping to improve the accessibility and clarity of exercise science notes while maintaining scientific accuracy.', 'temperature': 0.7}}
