tps://doi.org/[DOI]\n- Key finding: [Specific measurable result or main conclusion]\n- Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]  \n- Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n\n**Alternative format with PMID:**\n**Author et al. (Year).** Title. *Journal*, Volume(Issue), Pages. PMID: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/[PMID]\n- Key finding: [Specific measurable result or main conclusion]\n- Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]\n- Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n\n[REQUIREMENTS:]\n- Include ONLY citations with working DOI or PMID links\n- Verify links work before including them\n- Focus on 3-5 highest quality, most relevant studies\n- Remove any citations that cannot be verified with working links\n\n## Practical Applications\n[How to actually implement this - ADD if missing]\n[Specific examples, numbers, protocols]\n[Real-world scenarios]\n\n## Common Mistakes\n⚠️ [What people typically get wrong]\n⚠️ [Misconceptions to avoid]\n⚠️ [Implementation errors]\n\n## Related Concepts\n[PRESERVE ALL EXISTING LINKS EXACTLY]\n[Add contextual phrases around links where helpful]\n[Example: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]" instead of just "[[B2. Protein Requirements]]"]\n\n## Update Log\n- [Date]: [What changed and why]\n```\n\nLINKING PRESERVATION RULES:\n- Keep all [[Note Name]] links exactly as they appear\n- You can add descriptive text around links: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]"\n- Never modify what\'s inside the [[ ]] brackets\n- Never change link destinations\n\nRESEARCH CITATION RULES:\n- CRITICAL: Every citation MUST have a working DOI or PMID link - NO EXCEPTIONS\n- Use the ENHANCED RESEARCH EVIDENCE FORMAT with mandatory links\n- Format: **Author et al. (Year).** Title. *Journal*, Volume(Issue), Pages. DOI: https://doi.org/[DOI]\n- Follow each citation with:\n  - Key finding: [Specific measurable result]\n  - Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]\n  - Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n- VERIFY all DOI/PMID links work before including them\n- If you cannot find working links, do NOT include the citation\n- Better to have 2-3 citations with working links than 5 without links\n- Focus on highest quality studies with verifiable sources\n- Do NOT make up or guess DOI/PMID numbers\n\nHere\'s the note to rewrite:\n\n\\#\\#\\# A11. Biomechanics: Levers in the Human Body  \n\\*\\*ID:\\*\\* A11-BIOMECH-LEVERS  \n\\*\\*Created:\\*\\* 2025-04-23\n\nThe musculoskeletal system functions as a system of levers to create movement. Understanding lever classes helps analyze exercise mechanics and efficiency. A lever consists of a rigid bar (bone), a pivot point/fulcrum (joint), an effort force (muscle contraction), and a resistance force (load/gravity).\n\n\\- \\*\\*First-Class Lever:\\*\\* Fulcrum between effort and resistance (E \\- F \\- R). Example: Head nodding (atlanto-occipital joint \\= F, neck extensors \\= E, weight of head \\= R). Can provide mechanical advantage or disadvantage depending on distances.  \n\\- \\*\\*Second-Class Lever:\\*\\* Resistance between fulcrum and effort (F \\- R \\- E). Example: Calf raise (ball of foot \\= F, body weight \\= R, calf muscles \\= E). Always provides mechanical advantage (effort arm \\> resistance arm), favors force production over speed/ROM. Rare in the human body.  \n\\- \\*\\*Third-Class Lever:\\*\\* Effort between fulcrum and resistance (F \\- E \\- R). Example: Bicep curl (elbow joint \\= F, bicep insertion \\= E, weight in hand \\= R). Most common type in the human body. Always provides mechanical disadvantage (resistance arm \\> effort arm), favors speed and range of motion over force production. Requires muscles to generate high forces.\n\n\\*\\*Implications:\\*\\* Lever mechanics influence muscle force requirements, joint stress, and movement speed. Exercise selection and technique can alter effective lever arms.\n\n\\*\\*Key Research:\\*\\*  \n\\- Hamill J, Knutzen KM. (2014). Biomechanical Basis of Human Movement (4th ed.). Lippincott Williams & Wilkins.  \n\\- Neumann DA. (2016). Kinesiology of the Musculoskeletal System: Foundations for Rehabilitation (3rd ed.). Mosby Elsevier.\n\n\\*\\*Links:\\*\\* [[A12. Biomechanics Force Vectors]],[[A13. Biomechanics Torque and Movement]], [[A18. Proper Lifting Mechanics]], [[G-VII-2. Kinesiology Movement Analysis]]'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a fitness science expert helping to improve the accessibility and clarity of exercise science notes while maintaining scientific accuracy.', 'temperature': 0.7}}
