tps://doi.org/[DOI]\n- Key finding: [Specific measurable result or main conclusion]\n- Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]  \n- Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n\n**Alternative format with PMID:**\n**Author et al. (Year).** Title. *Journal*, Volume(Issue), Pages. PMID: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/[PMID]\n- Key finding: [Specific measurable result or main conclusion]\n- Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]\n- Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n\n[REQUIREMENTS:]\n- Include ONLY citations with working DOI or PMID links\n- Verify links work before including them\n- Focus on 3-5 highest quality, most relevant studies\n- Remove any citations that cannot be verified with working links\n\n## Practical Applications\n[How to actually implement this - ADD if missing]\n[Specific examples, numbers, protocols]\n[Real-world scenarios]\n\n## Common Mistakes\n⚠️ [What people typically get wrong]\n⚠️ [Misconceptions to avoid]\n⚠️ [Implementation errors]\n\n## Related Concepts\n[PRESERVE ALL EXISTING LINKS EXACTLY]\n[Add contextual phrases around links where helpful]\n[Example: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]" instead of just "[[B2. Protein Requirements]]"]\n\n## Update Log\n- [Date]: [What changed and why]\n```\n\nLINKING PRESERVATION RULES:\n- Keep all [[Note Name]] links exactly as they appear\n- You can add descriptive text around links: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]"\n- Never modify what\'s inside the [[ ]] brackets\n- Never change link destinations\n\nRESEARCH CITATION RULES:\n- CRITICAL: Every citation MUST have a working DOI or PMID link - NO EXCEPTIONS\n- Use the ENHANCED RESEARCH EVIDENCE FORMAT with mandatory links\n- Format: **Author et al. (Year).** Title. *Journal*, Volume(Issue), Pages. DOI: https://doi.org/[DOI]\n- Follow each citation with:\n  - Key finding: [Specific measurable result]\n  - Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]\n  - Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n- VERIFY all DOI/PMID links work before including them\n- If you cannot find working links, do NOT include the citation\n- Better to have 2-3 citations with working links than 5 without links\n- Focus on highest quality studies with verifiable sources\n- Do NOT make up or guess DOI/PMID numbers\n\nHere\'s the note to rewrite:\n\n\\#\\#\\# A13. Biomechanics: Torque and Movement  \n\\*\\*ID:\\*\\* A13-BIOMECH-TORQUE  \n\\*\\*Created:\\*\\* 2025-04-23\n\nTorque (or Moment of Force) is the rotational effect of a force. It causes or tends to cause rotation around an axis (joint). Torque \\= Force x Moment Arm.\n\n\\- \\*\\*Moment Arm (Lever Arm):\\*\\* The perpendicular distance from the axis of rotation (joint center) to the line of action of the force.  \n\\- \\*\\*Muscle Torque:\\*\\* Force generated by muscle x its moment arm relative to the joint center. This torque creates movement.  \n\\- \\*\\*Resistance Torque:\\*\\* Force of resistance (gravity, external load) x its moment arm relative to the joint center. This torque opposes movement.  \n\\- \\*\\*Net Torque:\\*\\* The sum of all torques acting around a joint. Determines the direction and acceleration of angular motion. If muscle torque \\> resistance torque, concentric contraction occurs; if resistance torque \\> muscle torque, eccentric contraction occurs; if torques are equal, isometric contraction occurs.  \n\\- \\*\\*Changing Moment Arms:\\*\\* Both muscle and resistance moment arms change throughout a joint\'s range of motion, altering the torque required and produced at different joint angles. This explains why exercises feel harder/easier at different points.\n\n\\*\\*Implications:\\*\\* Torque dictates the rotational demands on muscles and joints during exercise. Understanding how moment arms change helps explain strength curves and allows for exercise modification to target specific points in the ROM or alter difficulty.\n\n\\*\\*Key Research:\\*\\*  \n\\- Hamill J, Knutzen KM. (2014). Biomechanical Basis of Human Movement (4th ed.). Lippincott Williams & Wilkins.  \n\\- Zatsiorsky VM, Prilutsky BI. (2012). Biomechanics of Skeletal Muscles. Human Kinetics.\n\n\\*\\*Links:\\*\\* [[A11. Biomechanics Levers in the Human Body]], [[A12. Biomechanics Force Vectors]], [[A18. Proper Lifting Mechanics]], [[G-VII-2. Kinesiology Movement Analysis]]'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a fitness science expert helping to improve the accessibility and clarity of exercise science notes while maintaining scientific accuracy.', 'temperature': 0.7}}
