tps://doi.org/[DOI]\n- Key finding: [Specific measurable result or main conclusion]\n- Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]  \n- Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n\n**Alternative format with PMID:**\n**Author et al. (Year).** Title. *Journal*, Volume(Issue), Pages. PMID: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/[PMID]\n- Key finding: [Specific measurable result or main conclusion]\n- Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]\n- Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n\n[REQUIREMENTS:]\n- Include ONLY citations with working DOI or PMID links\n- Verify links work before including them\n- Focus on 3-5 highest quality, most relevant studies\n- Remove any citations that cannot be verified with working links\n\n## Practical Applications\n[How to actually implement this - ADD if missing]\n[Specific examples, numbers, protocols]\n[Real-world scenarios]\n\n## Common Mistakes\n⚠️ [What people typically get wrong]\n⚠️ [Misconceptions to avoid]\n⚠️ [Implementation errors]\n\n## Related Concepts\n[PRESERVE ALL EXISTING LINKS EXACTLY]\n[Add contextual phrases around links where helpful]\n[Example: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]" instead of just "[[B2. Protein Requirements]]"]\n\n## Update Log\n- [Date]: [What changed and why]\n```\n\nLINKING PRESERVATION RULES:\n- Keep all [[Note Name]] links exactly as they appear\n- You can add descriptive text around links: "adequate protein intake [[B2. Protein Requirements]]"\n- Never modify what\'s inside the [[ ]] brackets\n- Never change link destinations\n\nRESEARCH CITATION RULES:\n- CRITICAL: Every citation MUST have a working DOI or PMID link - NO EXCEPTIONS\n- Use the ENHANCED RESEARCH EVIDENCE FORMAT with mandatory links\n- Format: **Author et al. (Year).** Title. *Journal*, Volume(Issue), Pages. DOI: https://doi.org/[DOI]\n- Follow each citation with:\n  - Key finding: [Specific measurable result]\n  - Study type: [RCT/Meta-analysis/Systematic review/Observational/Case study]\n  - Evidence quality: [High/Medium/Low] ([Brief quality descriptor])\n- VERIFY all DOI/PMID links work before including them\n- If you cannot find working links, do NOT include the citation\n- Better to have 2-3 citations with working links than 5 without links\n- Focus on highest quality studies with verifiable sources\n- Do NOT make up or guess DOI/PMID numbers\n\nHere\'s the note to rewrite:\n\n### A19. Principle of Overload\n**ID:** A19-PRINCIPLE-OVERLOAD\n**Created:** 2025-04-25\n\n*(Content based on FZC-1.1)*\nThe Principle of Overload is the cornerstone of all physiological adaptation to exercise. It states that for the body to improve its function (e.g., increase strength, muscle size, endurance capacity), it must be subjected to a training stress (load, stimulus) that is greater than what it is currently accustomed to. This overload disrupts the body\'s internal balance (homeostasis), signaling the need for adaptation to better handle that stress in the future.\n\n**Methods of Applying Overload:**\n- **Intensity:** Increasing the resistance/weight lifted (%1RM), running/cycling speed, incline, or perceived exertion (RPE).\n- **Volume:** Increasing the total amount of work performed, typically by adding repetitions, sets, or increasing the duration/frequency of sessions.\n- **Density:** Increasing the amount of work done within a given time period, usually by decreasing rest intervals between sets or exercises.\n- **Frequency:** Increasing the number of training sessions per week for a specific muscle group or activity (must be balanced with recovery).\n- **Complexity:** Progressing to more challenging exercise variations requiring greater skill or stability.\n\n**Progressive Overload:** This principle must be applied progressively over time ([[A21. Principle of Progression]]). As the body adapts, the *same* stimulus no longer constitutes an overload. Therefore, the training stress must be systematically increased to continue driving adaptation. Insufficient overload leads to stagnation; excessive overload applied too quickly or without adequate recovery leads to maladaptation, burnout, or injury ([[M2. Overtraining and Functional Overreaching]]).\n\n**Key Research:**\n- Kraemer WJ, Ratamess NA. (2004). Fundamentals of resistance training: progression and exercise prescription. Medicine and Science in Sports and Exercise, 36(4), 674-688.\n- Fry AC, Kraemer WJ. (1997). Resistance exercise overtraining and overreaching. Neuroendocrine responses. Sports Medicine, 23(2), 106-129.\n- American College of Sports Medicine. (2021). ACSM\'s Guidelines for Exercise Testing and Prescription (11th ed.). Wolters Kluwer.\n\n**Links:** [[A21. Principle of Progression]], [[B1. Progressive Overload]], [[A8. Cardiovascular Physiology Fundamentals]] (Adaptations), [[A7. Muscle Physiology Fundamentals]] (Adaptations), [[M. Recovery Strategies]], [[M2. Overtraining and Functional Overreaching]]'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a fitness science expert helping to improve the accessibility and clarity of exercise science notes while maintaining scientific accuracy.', 'temperature': 0.7}}
